I'd recommend you learn the art how to safely use-test items. This is
the way conduct people do it. In your case, it also helps cut down on
things you really have to use the scroll of ID on before you can bless
it.

1) Artifact naming trick.

2) Curse-testing with a pet. Certain types of Rings, amulets, and
magical armor are more likely generated cursed, so you can conversely
get some information from their BUC status.

3) Price-ID, except possibly for wands. If the game is especially
cruel and Izchak's is the only store, you might have to throw things
in (fragiles tucked in a bag of holding) to get the buy quote. Credit
cloning will get your stuff back.

4) * Wands: Engrave-ID. Zap no-message wands at yourself.
  * Rings: wear-test noncursed ones for 1 turn. (You don't want
polymorph to shred your armor etc.)
  * Potions: Dip-test with a non-poisoned missile weapons, a poisoned
one, a unihorn, and the amethyst from mine's end. Apply-test oil.

5) Enlightenment for rings and amulets, if you have it. Try to wear
two rings of different price groups.

6) Now that you have grouped things and narrowed down the
possibilities, you can use-test for the useful stuff. Review your
discoveries list for what you saw monsters use.

That last step is really an art. So far, the best manual is David
Damerell's Object Identification FAQ. I've always meant to put
instructions on the wiki, but I haven't come around to doing it.
Partly because I'm not that sure where to best put it, and
specifically for potions, if anybody except illiterate pacifists
really needs detailed instructions.

When things are identified (or determined to be useless), the only
things that really need holy water before Gehennom are the bag of
holding, some good bones loot, and perhaps also the unihorn. So
there's no need to rush.

See http://nethack.wikia.com/wiki/Holy_water#Conduct_considerations
for more exotic ways to make holy water.


